															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1404533&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609202346" alt="Zyn" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1404533)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;" >Zyn</a></span> <span class="odate time_1609198918 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">28 Dec 2020 23:41</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4856102">
									

<blockquote>
<p>It's just a fact of baseline reality that this anomaly cannot be contained</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This might come off as just being overpowered to force the narrative to be what the author wants. It feels more contrived than, say, <a href="/scp-1915">SCP-1915</a> (Status Quo) where the anomaly is very difficult to contain, but the reality bending for that anomaly feels inherent to the character's story independent of any authorial meddling.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The overuse of resources and personnel is due to the fact that they can't get him to stay in containment for more than five minutes</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My main suspension of disbelief gripe is the Foundation taking so long and using up so many resources before they figure out what they need to do. If you really want to go that route, it's your prerogative as author; however, I would not be greenlighting a premise that relies on that narrative setup.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The character can also be reduced from an arrogant parody of Mary Sues to someone who really, really doesn't want to be locked up.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This approach would be much more workable&#8212;fear of being confined is more relatable to the average reader.</p>

							</div>

								
					
